      "text": "**Solution 1**\n\n1. Let $z = re^{i\\theta}$ be a complex number in circle $C$ with center at the origin. Since $ABCDEF$ is a regular hexagon with center $O$ and equal sides of one unit,\n   - $A = re^{i\\cdot(60^\\circ+360^\\circ k)} = re^{i(120^\\circ+360^\\circ k)}$\n   - $B = re^{i(60^\\circ+60^\\circ k)} = re^{i(180^\\circ+360^\\circ k)}$\n   - $C = re^{i(60^\\circ+120^\\circ k)} = re^{i(180^\\circ+120^\\circ k)}$\n   - $D = re^{i(60^\\circ+210^\\circ k)} = -re^{i(240^\\circ+360^\\circ k)}$\n   - $E = re^{i(60^\\circ+240^\\circ k)} = -re^{i(300^\\circ+360^\\circ k)}$\n   - $F = re^{i(60^\\circ+300^\\circ k)} = -re^{i(360^\\circ+360^\\circ k)}$\n2. The center 3 unit away from vertex $B$ is $g = -ei$\n3. The area of C can be found by integrating $f(x) = \\sqrt{x^2+1}$ from $x=-1$ to $x=1$, taking account of the complex zeros located at $g, 1+2g, e$, and $1$ (due to symmetry):\n   \\[\n   C = 2\\cdot \\underset{g=(-1,1)}{\\overset{-1}{\\int}}\\displaystyle \\sqrt{x^2+1}\\, dx+2\\cdot \\underset{-2g=f(i,-1)}{\\overset{0}{\\int}}\\displaystyle \\sqrt{x^2+1}\\, dx+2\\cdot \\underset{e=g+1,f(1)=0}{\\overset{2}{\\int}}\\displaystyle \\sqrt{x^2+1}\\, dx+2\\cdot \\underset{-eg+1=0}{\\overset{2}{\\int}}\\displaystyle \\sqrt{x^2+1}\\, dx\n   \\]\n4. This simplifies to\n   \\[\n   = 2\\underset{-1}{\\overset{1}{\\int}}\\displaystyle \\sqrt{x^2+1}\\, dx+2\\underset{1}{\\overset{1+2}{\\int}}\\displaystyle \\sqrt{x^2+1}\\, dx+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 3\n   \\]\n5. This only includes one integral for $ g \\in \\{f(i,-1), e\\}$.\n\nThis integral is\n\\[\n= 2 + 4 + 2 \\log(2) = 8 + 2 \\log(2)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of hexagon C is:\n$$\\boxed{16\\log(2)+T(8+2\\log(2))}$$.\n\n**Solution 2**\n\n1. The area enclosed by the bounding curve of a regular hexagon is given by:\n   \\[\n   S = 2 |S_{\\text{triangle}} + S_{\\text{segment}}|\n   \\]\n2. The area of the bounding triangle $S_{\\text{triangle}} = 0.5 |a_1 - a_2|$\n3. Here, $a_1$ is the length of one side, and $a_2$ is the hypotenuse of the isosceles triangle $a_2^2 = 1^2 + 1^2 = 2$, hence the area $S_{\\text{triangle}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n4. The area of the bounded segment is given by:\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{segment}} = (a_1 - a_2) \\cdot (\\frac{a_1}{2} + \\frac{a_2}{2}) \\pi + \\pi\n   \\]\n5. In this case, the area $S_{\\text{segment}} = 1 \\cdot 2 \\pi + \\pi = 3 \\pi$\n6. Therefore, $S = |0.5 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} + 3\\pi| \\approx 5.08$\n7. Hence, $S = 2 \\cdot |S_{\\text{triangle}} + S_{\\text{segment}}| = |0.5 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} + 3\\pi| = 5.85$.\n\n**Note to the audience**\n\nCareful reading of the problem and understanding of the shape are crucial, as visualizing a pure algebraic problem can be trickier than expected.",
