      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Start by rewriting the area of $R$ in the complex plane as an integral of the arc length of the two sides. Let $z = x + iy$; this side has endpoints $z = -1$ and $z = 1$ in the complex plane. The arc of the right angle is $|z+x| dA = |\\sqrt{(y - 1)^2 + 1} + \\sqrt{1 - y^2}| dA$ where $x$ is free; then we can integrate this to obtain the first part. A linear change of variable, $2u = 1 - y < 1$:\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\int_{-1}^1 |\\sqrt{(y-1)^2 + 1} + \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}| dA = \\bigg|  \\int_{-1/2}^1 \\sqrt{(2u -1)^2 + 1} d(2u)\\bigg| = \\underbrace{\\big(  \\int_{-1/2}^1\\sqrt{(2u-1)^2 + 1} +\\sqrt{1- (2u - 1)^2} d(2u)\\big)}_{\\text{ODR}} \\\\ \n&\\leftarrow\n\\Rightarrow\n\\int_{-1/2}^1 \\sqrt{(2u-1)^2 + 1} d(2u) + \\int_{-1/2}^1 \\sqrt{1 - (2u-1)^2}  d(2u)\n\\end{align*}\nIt only takes a minute to plot these integrals. The second is sketchy at the left edge and there: $1 - (2u-1)^2$ will be negative in that range and guarantee a negative sign. We are left with one main integral. Use the half-angle formula for sine, noticing that $|\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{3})| = 2^{-1/2}$, before finally evaluating it using the linearity of the integral. Finally, finding the area of $S$, we'll have to multiply the area of half of the square with  $\\frac{a}{2}$ at the end. $\\boxed{36}$ $\\blacksquare$ Nuts and Bolts: Whenever you are asked to find the area of an unbounded region, e.g. $S = \\lbrace \\frac{1}{z}|z \\in R \\rbrace$ with $R$ being a bounded region, consider the transformation $z \\to \\frac1{z}$, as it will eliminate the unbounded region in the denominator.\n\nTo break those complexities down, make the following substitutions into the original equation:\n\n$I = \\int_{R}1 dz = \\int_R \\frac{z}{|z|^2} dA$, where $z=x+iy$ and R is bounded\n\nNow, if you rewrite $|z|^2$ and reorder the equation:\n\n$I = -\\int_{R'} \\frac{x^2}{|z|^2}dA - \\int_{R'} \\frac{xy}{|z|^2}dA$\n\nNow now, use alternate therms, where $R' = \\{(x, y)| x < 0 \\ , |x+y| < 1 \\}$\n\nBoth of the above integrals have a $dA = dx dy$ component where the $x-y$ forms the left-half plane, now the substitution is much clearer:\n\n$\\iint x^2 \\ dA = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$\n$\\iint xy\\ dA  = \\frac{\\pi}{8}$\n\nCombining the second half of the process, thanks to another doubling of the region and 'change of variable' term we have the following, now with the answer already found and put in $x^2,y^2$ space:\n\n$\\int_{x^2+y^2 < 1}\\\\ \\left(1 \\times \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + \\left(\\sqrt{1-x^2}^2 \\times \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$\n$\\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n$\\boxed{36}$",
